  "AI content payment is not merely a copyright or transaction-efficiency problem; it is a question of political-economic legitimacy. As AI valuations reach hundreds of billions, society asks why the gains from public web content and human knowledge accrue to a few firms. The paper proposes a six-layer tiered data market — from free public content through scarce verified datasets to public benefit-sharing — and defines the AI network democratic economy: rights, pricing, participation, auditability, and returns, each democratized through machine-readable protocols like AICR/AICL.": "Tefo ya diteng tša AI ga se bothata bja ditokelo tša tsopolo goba bokgoni bja tshepedišo feela; ke potšišo ya tumelelo ya ekonomi-polotiki. Ge ditheko tša AI di fihla go di-billion tše makgolo, setšhaba se botšiša gore ke ka baka la eng dipoelo tša diteng tša inthanete ya phatlalatša le tsebo ya batho di iša feela go dikhamphani tše sego kae. Pampiri ye e šišinya mmaraka wa data wa magora a tshela wa maemo — go tloga diteng tša mahala tša phatlalatša, go putulla go tša dikologanyo tša data tše di hlwahlilwego, go ya go go abelana mohola wa setšhaba — gomme e hlaloša ekonomi ya network ya AI ya temokrasi: ditokelo, go beelwa ditheko, tsenelelo, kgonagalo ya tlhahlobo, le dipoelo, tše dingwe le tše dingwe di dirwa tša temokrasi ka diphrothokholo tše di balwago ke metšhene tša go swana le AICR/AICL.",
